<LessonLayout title="Penance and Reconciliation" cic={lessonRef} scripture={lessonScripture}>

<section class="content">

<h2>Big Question</h2>

<p class="big-question">Can God forgive anything? Even what I did?</p>


<p>
The prophet Nathan stood before King David, and instead of a direct accusation, he told a story. "There were two men in a city," he said, "one rich and one poor. The rich man had many flocks, but when a traveler came, he took the poor man's single, beloved lamb and prepared it for his guest." David burned with anger. "That man deserves to die!" he shouted. And Nathan said: "You are that man" (2 Sam 12:1-7). David, the king who had taken Bathsheba and had her husband killed, saw himself in the mirror of the story. He fell to his knees. "I have sinned against the Lord," he said. And the prophet answered: "The Lord has put away your sin." A single moment of truth-telling changed everything. That is the power of Reconciliation: a space where the truth does not destroy us but sets us free.
</p>


</section>

<section class="content">

<h2>Understanding the Mystery</h2>

<p>The Church teaches us through the Catechism.</p>

<div class="depth-box depth-box--semilla">
